{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789906749,"nonce":"xK3kYutKaVCLuWuqYZpg8NAR9B0=","title":"A frame interprets for as long as its outlined region takes to build","message":"With resume points outlined (9f264cf), a frame that warms asks at its\n16th header visit and keeps interpreting until the region is optimised\nand compiled. fannkuch on the NUC, tiered with LLVM: the ask at 37.9 ms\nafter start, the region published at 51.7 ms (the whole pipeline over\nthe region 8.7 ms, Cranelift 3.7 ms), the transfer right after. Before\noutlining the ask came at 59.2 ms, because every body with a loop was\noptimised before its first run, and the resume point followed 3.8 ms\nlater. The wall is 40 to 60 ms better; the first iteration reads 61 to\n65 ms against 47, since the 14 ms of interpretation now fall inside it\nwhere the 21 ms of up-front optimisation fell before it. nbody +5 ms,\nmeteor +5 ms, float +4 ms on the first iteration for the same reason.\n\nA hot function is optimised twice: its region for the frame, then its\nbody for the calls after (ensure_baseline), on the same thread, one\nafter the other, so the body and its LLVM promotion come ~10 ms later\nthan before (meteor it2 55 vs 51 ms).\n\nLevers, in order of promise:\n- Fewer sweep rounds for a region. It is tier-0 code and the LLVM tier\n  optimises it again; the sweep runs to a fixed point (up to 8 rounds).\n- Ask earlier: OSR_WARM_VISITS is 16; the interpretation between visit\n  4 and 16 is the bulk of the wait for a loop whose iterations are long.\n  Costs a speculative region for loops that never get hot.\n- Optimise the body from the region's work rather than from scratch,\n  or compile the LLVM tier of the body without the HIR pipeline.","files":null}]}